Welcome to the quaydoc manual. This site is built by quaydoc itself: the
source lives under `examples/` and the rendered output is what you are
reading.

## What is quaydoc

quaydoc is a documentation site generator written in pure standard-library
Python. It reads a tree of plain-text pages using a small markup language,
renders HTML, and publishes a search index, a sitemap and an Atom feed. It
has no third-party runtime dependencies and no daemons: `quaydoc build`
produces a static directory you can copy anywhere.

The manual is organised like this:

- [[Installation]] shows how to set up quaydoc,
- [[Writing pages]] is the markup reference,
- [[Links and references]] explains internal links and the table of contents,
- [[Building a site]] covers configuration and the build pipeline,
- [[Command-line reference]] documents every command,
- [[Theming]] describes the default theme and custom themes,
- [[Search and feeds]] covers the search index, sitemap and Atom feed,
- [[Troubleshooting]] collects common problems.

## Quick start

```console
$ cd examples
$ python3 -m quaydoc.cli build .
$ python3 -m quaydoc.cli check site
No issues.
$ python3 -m quaydoc.cli serve site --port 8000
```

[tip] The `check` command validates every fragment link in the built site;
most issues reported in the wild are caught there first.

## Conventions

Pages are plain UTF-8 text. Front matter at the top of a file sets the
title, order, date, tags and draft state:

```
---
title: Installation
order: 10
date: 2025-01-08
tags: [setup, install]
---
```

Everything after the closing `---` is page content. Headings are set with
`#` through `######`; the table of contents panel lists every heading in
the page, and headings are reachable as `#anchor` fragments whose spelling
is documented in [[Links and references]].
